  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Betsileo\"\nsubtitle: \"Madagascar\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Betsileo are a highland ethnic group of Madagascar. |\n| Where | They live in the southern Madagascar plateau, mainly around Fianarantsoa. |\n| How many | They are Madagascar’s third-largest ethnic group by population; about 94% are Christians. |\n| Language | They speak a dialect of Malagasy, a Malayo-Polynesian language. |\n| Religion | Most are Protestant or Catholic, alongside continuing ancestral traditions. |\n| Known for | Rice terraces · Zebu raising · Woodcarving · Woven textiles · Ceremonies combining Christian and ancestral traditions |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Betsileo live in Madagascar’s southern highlands. Their culture is closely tied to rice farming, terraced fields, cattle raising and woven goods. They combine Christian practices with older beliefs about ancestors and spirits. Their kingdoms also had oral traditions dating back to the 17th century.\n\n## Material culture\n\nBetsileo material culture includes woven cotton, basketry, vegetable-fibre hats, wooden tools, pottery and carved objects.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nBetsileo textiles use cotton, stripes, geometric designs, fringes and embroidery.\n\n- **Woven cotton textiles** — Cotton cloths include striped and geometric patterns, fringes and embroidery.\n- **Burial cloths** — Burial cloths are cotton textiles with wide stripes, thin stripes and geometric patterns.\n- **Striped Betsileo tissue** — This tissue is sewn at the middle, striped in different colours, embroidered with silk and finished with fringed warp threads.\n- **Brown embroidered cloth** — This rectangular brown cotton cloth has fringe on every side and a silver geometric design with the initials “EB”.\n- **Basketry** — Basketry is used for hats, covers, mats and baskets.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nRecorded Betsileo dress includes woven waist-bands, fibre hats, grass caps and decorated cloths.\n\n- **Cotton waist-band** — A waist-band is woven from cotton and finished with braided bands.\n- **Basketry hats** — Several hats are made from basketry.\n- **Vegetable-fibre hat** — One recorded hat is made from vegetable fibre.\n- **Plaited-grass cap** — A recorded cap is made from plaited grass.\n- **Cotton burial cloths** — Cotton burial cloths use stripes, fringes, geometric patterns and embroidery.\n\n### Architecture\n\nTraditional Betsileo homes included vegetable-fibre huts and wooden huts for nobles, while mud and brick houses are more common today.\n\n- **Vegetable-fibre huts** — Common traditional huts were made of vegetable fibre.\n- **Nobles’ wooden huts** — Wooden huts were reserved for nobles.\n- **Zebu-horn decoration** — Huts could be decorated with motifs or zebu horns.\n- **Rice terraces** — Rice paddies cover the sides of the hills and valleys around Fianarantsoa in elaborate terraces.\n- **Mud and brick houses** — Mud and brick houses are more common today.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nBetsileo everyday objects include pottery, woodwork, gourds, basketry, leaf items and simple tools.\n\n- **Double-gourd water flask** — This water flask is made of burnished red pottery and shaped like a double gourd.\n- **Wooden tableware** — Recorded wooden objects include a spoon, plate and shallow dish.\n- **Gourd bottle** — A gourd bottle is among the recorded everyday objects.\n- **Pandanus-leaf cup** — A cup is made from pandanus leaves.\n- **Horn tinder-box** — A tinder-box is made from horn, wood, cord and bone.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nThe recorded Betsileo body adornment is a pair of silver earrings with coral studs.\n\n- **Silver coral-stud earrings** — The earrings are silver and set with coral studs.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nRecorded Betsileo instruments include the valiha and several reed, bark and bamboo instruments.\n\n- **Bamboo tube zither** (*Valiha*) — The valiha is a bamboo tube zither or guitar with strings made from bamboo bark or peel.\n- **Reed flute** — Reed flutes are among the recorded Betsileo instruments.\n- **Bark flute** — Bark flutes are also recorded.\n- **Bamboo flute** — Bamboo flutes are also recorded.\n- **Snuff-box instrument** — An unfinished reed or bamboo instrument is described as a snuff-box.\n\n## Dance & theatre\n\nThe sources do not describe Betsileo dances, theatre or staged entertainment.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nBetsileo ritual life combines Christian practice with ancestral traditions, including the famadihana ceremony.\n\n- **Turning of the bones** (*famadihana*) — During famadihana, ancestors’ remains are taken from tombs, wrapped in new linens and celebrated.\n- **Creator deity** (*Zanahary*) — Zanahary is a creator deity who is still invoked for ceremonies.\n- **Divination** — Diviners and astrologists are consulted to set ceremony dates and read people’s futures.\n- **Witch-doctor practice** — Witch doctors are believed to manipulate magic and communicate with ancestors.\n- **Ancestral and Christian practice** — Christian practices continue alongside ancestral traditions and religious beliefs.\n\n## Foodways\n\nRice is the Betsileo staple and economic mainstay, usually eaten with livestock and farm crops.\n\n- **Rice** — Rice is cultivated widely and is the main staple food.\n- **Livestock with rice** — Rice is commonly eaten with beef, chicken or duck.\n- **Cassava and beans** — Cassava and beans are secondary crops eaten with rice.\n- **Farm vegetables** — Other farm vegetables also accompany rice.\n- **Rice terraces** — Rice fields are built into terraces in the Betsileo region.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nBetsileo kingdoms had oral traditions dating back to the 17th century.\n\n- **Fandriana kingdom** (*Fandriana*) — Fandriana was one of the Betsileo kingdoms with old oral traditions.\n- **Fisakana kingdom** (*Fisakana*) — Fisakana was one of the Betsileo kingdoms with old oral traditions.\n- **Manandriana kingdom** (*Manandriana*) — Manandriana was one of the Betsileo kingdoms with old oral traditions.\n- **Isandra kingdom** (*Isandra*) — Isandra was one of the Betsileo kingdoms with old oral traditions.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nThe Betsileo speak a Malagasy dialect and combine Christian practice with beliefs about ancestors and spirits.\n\n- **Malagasy language** (*Malagasy*) — Malagasy is a Malayo-Polynesian language derived from the Barito languages of southern Borneo.\n- **Ancestral spirits** — Earlier Betsileo beliefs recognized ancestral spirits.\n- **Ghosts** — Earlier beliefs also recognized ghosts.\n- **Nature spirits** — Nature spirits were part of earlier Betsileo beliefs.\n- **Christianity** — About 94% of Betsileo are Christians, most of them Protestant or Catholic.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *Valiha* — Bamboo tube zither\n- *famadihana* — Turning of the bones ceremony\n- *Zanahary* — Creator deity\n- *Fandriana* — Betsileo kingdom\n- *Fisakana* — Betsileo kingdom\n- *Manandriana* — Betsileo kingdom\n- *Isandra* — Betsileo kingdom\n- *Betsileo* — Highland ethnic group of Madagascar\n- *Malagasy* — Language spoken by the Betsileo\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- The sources used do not provide books with author, title, publisher and year for further reading.\n- The sources used do not identify a named scholar or documentation project for Betsileo culture.\n- [Betsileo people — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Betsileo_people)\n- The UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage source supplied for this profile lists no inscriptions for Madagascar.\n- The sources used do not provide a Smithsonian Folkways search link.\n- The museum catalogue records supplied identify holdings of the British Museum, the Museum of World Culture and the Museum of Ethnography, but do not provide collection URLs.\n\n",
